{
 "id": "baselines-no-gm2017-2026-10-11",
 "title": "Random Forest Baseline",
 "date": "2026-10-11",
 "summary": "Random forest, with gradient boosting for comparison, predicting LiDAR snow depth from UAVSAR, terrain and canopy at seven SnowEx sites. Each site is scored by models that never saw it. Grand Mesa's 2017 surveys are excluded. This is the reference every later model has to beat.",
 "findings": [
  "Random pixel splits overstate skill, and more so at coarser blocks: scored the old way, R\u00b2 rises from 0.63 at 3 m to 0.90 at 99 m, while on unseen sites the median R\u00b2 stays between \u22120.14 and +0.09.",
  "On unseen sites the random forest's RMSE is 0.45\u20130.57 m, 10\u201313% lower than predicting the training sites' mean depth.",
  "Most error is each site's overall snow amount: in the gradient-boosting maps the bias runs from \u22120.40 m (Little Cottonwood) to +0.66 m (Grand Mesa). The spatial pattern is partly captured: correlation 0.82 at Mores Creek, 0.69 at Dry Creek and 0.64 at Banner Summit, about 0.2 at Fraser and Little Cottonwood.",
  "The models barely use the radar: at 30 m terrain carries 0.75 of the importance, canopy 0.18 and radar 0.07, and unwrapped phase almost none. Removing every radar input does not raise the random forest's error (0.48 m without, 0.49 m with). One radar pair measures about a week of change, while the labels are absolute depth.",
  "Gradient boosting is worse than the random forest in this run (RMSE 0.55 m vs 0.49 m at 30 m), mostly at Grand Mesa, so its extra complexity is not justified.",
  "Grand Mesa's five 2017 surveys are excluded. About 70% of those rows have no canopy input and most of their radar scenes are HH-only; scored on them, the models showed no skill (pattern correlation 0.05 in a 6,000-row check). Excluding them slightly raised the error at the other six sites (0.49 to 0.51 m)."
 ],
 "training": {
  "evaluation": "Leave-one-site-out: each of the 7 labelled sites is predicted by models trained only on the other 6, so every number and map shows generalisation to unseen ground. Reynolds Creek has no snow-depth labels and is not used.",
  "data": "SnowEx Field Atlas v1.0.05 (enrichment 3.3.0). One sample = one block of 3 m cells (3, 6, 15, 30, 60 or 99 m; cell means, kept when >=80% of cells have snow depth and radar geometry) x one LiDAR snow-depth date x one UAVSAR interferometric pair within 5 days. 10 site-surveys on 8 LiDAR dates across 7 sites. Grand Mesa's five 2017 surveys are excluded (about 70% of those rows have no canopy input and most of their radar scenes are HH-only), so Grand Mesa is trained and scored on its 2020 survey only.",
  "features": [
   "Radar, per polarisation HH / HV / VV: amplitude pass 1 and 2 (dB), amplitude ratio (dB), coherence, unwrapped phase re-referenced to each scene's median, wrapped phase as cos/sin",
   "Geometry: local and flat incidence angle",
   "Terrain: elevation, slope, aspect as sin/cos",
   "Canopy: vegetation height, canopy fraction (nearest vegetation date)",
   "Timing: radar pair length, LiDAR-radar gap (days)"
  ],
  "models": [
   "Random forest: 300 trees, min_samples_leaf 10, max_features 0.33 (fixed settings)",
   "Gradient boosting (scikit-learn HistGradientBoosting, 300 iterations, L2 1.0): learning rate 0.05/0.1 and 15/31 leaves chosen by grouped cross-validation over the training sites only"
  ],
  "sampling": "Up to 60,000 training rows per site, sites weighted equally so Grand Mesa cannot dominate. The held-out site is never used for fitting, tuning or early stopping.",
  "reference": "Every model is compared with predicting the training mean; the old evaluation style (random 80/20 pixel split, all sites pooled) is run on the same rows for contrast.",
  "code": "scripts/ml/ in the cryogars-atlas-results repository.",
  "archive_url": "https://github.com/cryogars/cryogars-atlas/tree/v1.0.05"
 },
 "limits": [
  "Labels are absolute snow depth while one radar pair measures about a week of change, so terrain and canopy dominate these baselines.",
  "Mean R\u00b2 is not a headline number here. R\u00b2 compares a model with the held-out site's own mean depth, so a site with a narrow range of depths and an error in overall level scores far below zero (Grand Mesa 2020 \u2248 \u221211, Fraser \u2248 \u22122) and dominates the average. RMSE, error vs predicting the mean and median R\u00b2 are reported instead; mean R\u00b2 is listed in the full table.",
  "Each block appears once per matched radar pair; prediction maps average those pairs.",
  "Values are block means of the 3 m archive, not native-resolution radar."
 ],
